title: Six tests, run in order
subtitle: Test 2 must pass before any refusal below it means anything
width: 1150
---
1. An unsigned request is refused
   [ok]  403 -- "User: anonymous ... no network-based policy allows this"

2. A signed request from the named role succeeds
   [ok]  200 -- the allowed identity, the allowed method
         Nothing below means anything until this passes. Every call would
         be refused if the plumbing were simply broken, and a broken build
         produces a perfect-looking set of denials.

3. The SAME code, signed as a different role, is refused
   [ok]  403 -- arn:...assumed-role/week23-lattice-stranger/...
         Same VPC. Same two subnets. Same security group. Both roles are
         granted vpc-lattice-svcs:Invoke in their own IAM policy, so the
         refusal can only have come from the auth policy.
         This is the week.

4. The allowed role, using a method the policy does not permit
   [ok]  403 -- the RequestMethod condition is enforced, not decorative

5. A restrictive policy on a service whose auth type is NONE
   [FAIL] 200 -- wrong role, wrong method, served anyway
   [FAIL] the policy reports state: Inactive while still attached
          and the service network policy kept refusing anonymous callers
          throughout, so the system never looked broken

6. Auth type AWS_IAM with the policy deleted
   [ok]  403 -- "no service-based policy allows this action"
         Fails closed. Note "service-based" here against "network-based"
         in test 1: the error names which layer refused.

--- what the tests found that reading the code did not --------------------

  [FAIL] the service DNS resolves to IPv6; an IPv4-only VPC gets
         "Cannot assign requested address", which reads like routing
  [FAIL] the VPC association security group needs INBOUND rules, even
         though the client is making an outbound call. Egress-only
         produces a silent timeout, no error anywhere
  [FAIL] Lattice requires x-amz-content-sha256 inside the signature, set
         to the literal UNSIGNED-PAYLOAD. A real hash is rejected with
         "Signed payloads are not supported" -- a 403 from a service
         whose whole job is issuing 403s
